<h1>How it works</h1>

<p class="lead">Inputs, a section through the room, a result as a tendency. This page explains what is calculated, what is assumed and where the model stops.</p>

<p class="lang-note">The app comes in German and English and follows the language of your iPhone.</p>

<h2>What is calculated</h2>

<p>The app does not draw the air flow by hand. It calculates air flow and temperature. For the room, it keeps working out air flow and temperature in the section: how warm air rises, how cool air sinks and how the air moves around a curtain or a sofa. For the wall, it calculates how heat flows through each layer of the wall and how warm the inner surface stays, with room air regulated at 20 °C (common assumption); the wall view does not use the colors or the movement of the air. From the room air temperature and humidity, it calculates the dew point, and from the inner surface temperature the humidity at the surface.</p>

<p>The model is two-dimensional. While the radiator keeps up, the room air is held at the temperature set for the scene. Its limits are listed below.</p>

<h2>1. Inputs</h2>

<p>In the room view you choose the window, the wall, the radiator setting, the outside temperature, the room size and a curtain or a sofa at fixed positions in front of the window. In the wall view you choose the outdoor temperature, the relative humidity in the room and a wall template (for example an uninsulated wall or one insulated on the outside). The view shows the result for the open area of the outside wall and, in one line, for the outside edge.</p>

<h2>2. The 2D section</h2>

<p>The app calculates two things, separately:</p>

<ul>
    <li><strong>Wall surfaces.</strong> The temperature of the wall surface comes from heat conduction in a settled state through the wall, calculated as a case with common boundary conditions. At an outside edge the calculation is two-dimensional. A real room corner is usually colder than this edge.</li>
    <li><strong>Air in the room.</strong> The air movement, such as the circulation above the radiator or cool air sinking at the window, is a two-dimensional picture. Real room air moves in every direction and less evenly than in the model. This layer therefore shows mechanisms, not numbers. The colors show warmer and cooler air on a fixed °C scale. They are smoothed over a few minutes, so they follow changes with a delay. The short strokes show which way the air is moving right now; their warm or cool tint follows the same smoothed colors.</li>
</ul>


<h2>3. The result</h2>

<p>For the wall surface, the app shows a risk estimate in three levels with a range, based on common design values. It shows where the wall gets critically cool in the model.</p>

<h2>The physics inside</h2>

<p><strong>Room air.</strong> Air flow and temperature are calculated, not drawn. The app keeps working out how warm air rises above the radiator, how cool air sinks at the window and how the air moves around a curtain or a sofa, in a section through the middle of the room, live on the iPhone. Curtain and sofa are solid obstacles: the air has to go around them, or, for the curtain, can pass only a little through the fabric.</p>

<p><strong>How reliable it is.</strong> We checked the calculation core for the room air against a recognized reference case from the technical literature, at a higher resolution than the live view. That checks the calculation core, not the live view: it calculates at a lower resolution so that it runs smoothly on the iPhone, and real room air moves in every direction and less evenly than in the model. The view therefore shows trends, not values for your room. It has not been compared with real rooms.</p>

<p><strong>Wall.</strong> The wall view takes the wall template and the outside temperature from the room view and calculates a settled state with room air regulated at 20 °C (common assumption); it does not take the air temperature from the air flow in the room view. The heat transfer at the inner surface covers both air and radiation (common assumption). The wall is calculated layer by layer with heat conduction in a settled state, each layer with its own thermal conductivity and a range. The outside edge comes from a two-dimensional heat conduction calculation done in advance for each wall template. From the room air temperature and humidity, the app calculates the dew point. From the inner surface temperature, it calculates the humidity at the surface.</p>


<h2>Where the values come from</h2>

<p>Every number in the app is labelled:</p>

<ul>
    <li><strong>calculated</strong> – a result of the simulation from your inputs,</li>
    <li><strong>common assumption</strong> – a widely used design value, for example for heat transfer at surfaces,</li>
    <li><strong>assumption by the RoomXDynamics team</strong> – a value we have set as long as no reliable source is available. The app says so.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Limits</h2>

<ul>
    <li>Two dimensions instead of three. Corners, roller shutter boxes and junctions in the room are missing.</li>
    <li>Settled state: no daily cycle, no airing with open windows, no heat storage in the wall.</li>
    <li>The wall is a template, not the real build-up of a particular house.</li>
    <li>The air movement is qualitative. The app does not derive statements about draughts in a real room from it.</li>
    <li>Swirls: single swirls come and go. What shows the trend is the overall direction of the air.</li>
    <li>Internal insulation is not covered. It needs a different kind of calculation, with moisture moving through the wall.</li>
    <li>Furniture in front of the wall is not covered in the wall view.</li>
    <li>The wall behind the radiator is not calculated. Depending on how it is built, it can be warmer or colder there than the open area of the wall.</li>
</ul>

<p class="notice">If you can see mold: have the wall looked at on site by a professional.</p>

